// BUGS #128 guard: `++` and `--` are POSTFIX ONLY, and prefix use is rejected by
// name rather than silently parsed.
//
// Adding the `++` / `--` tokens makes the lexer maximal-munch, so `++a` scans as
// TkPlusPlus TkIdent — it can no longer be read as `+(+a)`. Something must decide
// what that stream means, and the two bad answers are (a) accept it as a synonym
// for postfix, which in statement position it genuinely is, or (b) fall through
// to "expected a literal, name or '(' here", a diagnostic that points at the
// operator and says nothing about why.
//
// It is rejected because prefix would be a second spelling for one behaviour.
// Statement position discards the value, so `++a` and `a++` are identical there;
// and where the value is wanted, the pre-increment value is `a + 1`, which reads
// better than either. `--a` is worse still: in a language without C's operators
// that text already means `-(-a)`, so accepting prefix `--` would make one
// spelling mean two things depending on a lexer decision the reader cannot see.
//
// Postfix in every position, including as an expression, is covered by
// test/pass/increment.sf.
//
// Expected: "'++' is postfix only in Saffron" and exit 1.

var a: Int = 1
++a
IO.println(a)
